<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Recycling for Our World – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short reports on classroom experiments or projects. Interactive: Write phrases or sentences for a group story or project with support or guidance.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a simple report on a project to describe the activity and its purpose (e.g., "Our poster is about recycling plastic bottles." "We explain how to recycle paper and bottles."). Can write sentences about recycling as part of a group experiment or project (e.g., "We recycle paper."). Can describe what they learned (e.g., "Recycling helps the Earth.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a classroom recycling bin with different items (plastic bottles, paper). Students brainstorm words they see or remember about recycling from previous lessons (e.g., "plastic," "bottles," "recycle," "bin," "save"). The teacher writes key words on the board. Then, students complete the sentence "We can recycle __________." orally, one by one. This activates previous vocabulary related to the theme "We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day." and prepares for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple sentences on the board using the brainstormed vocabulary and the present simple. For example, the teacher writes: "We recycle plastic bottles. We put them in the bin. Recycling helps the Earth." The teacher says each sentence aloud while writing, focusing on clear pronunciation of /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "save." The teacher points out the structure: Subject + Verb + Object.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we recycle in our classroom?" (Expected: "Plastic bottles," "paper.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does recycling help our planet?" (Expected: "Yes, it helps the Earth.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher may also ask, "Can we recycle plastic bottles?" to reinforce the modal verb 'can'.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple written paragraph (2-3 sentences) describing a classroom recycling activity. For example: "Our class has a recycling program. We classify plastic bottles and paper. There is a blue bin for plastic bottles. We can save the environment. Recycling is easy." The text is displayed on a projector or a large chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehending written information about recycling actions and their purpose. Students identify key information within the text to prepare for their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected: "Recycling" or "our recycling program.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read the text silently. The teacher reads statements and students say "True" or "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Our class recycles food scraps." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We classify plastic bottles." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Recycling helps the environment." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using present simple, modal verb 'can', 'there is/are', and target vocabulary: <i>plastic, bottles, recycling, bin, environment, recycle, save, easy</i>. Phonemic awareness: teacher highlights segmenting "environment" and "sustainability" while spelling words for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences related to the theme. They fill in the blanks using a word bank containing target vocabulary (e.g., "recycle," "bottles," "bin," "Earth," "can," "is").
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We ______ plastic ______." (recycle, bottles)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "There ______ a recycling ______." (is, bin)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We ______ help the ______." (can, Earth/environment)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Scramble and Rewrite):</b> The teacher provides scrambled sentences on the board or individual cards. Students work in pairs to unscramble the words and write the correct sentences in their notebooks. This reinforces sentence structure and present simple.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "bottles / plastic / recycle / we" -> "We recycle plastic bottles."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "environment / save / can / we / the" -> "We can save the environment."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for correct use of present simple and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Guided Sentence Writing (Picture Prompts):</b> Students look at 3-4 simple pictures showing different recycling actions (e.g., someone putting a plastic bottle in a bin, a recycling center, a child making something new from recycled material). For each picture, students write one or two simple sentences describing the action, using present simple and modal 'can'. The teacher models the first one: "Picture 1 shows a blue bin. There is a blue bin for plastic." Students are encouraged to use words like "sort," "classify," "protect," "reuse."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students write a short, simple report (2-3 sentences) about their classroom recycling efforts. This directly addresses the specific objective: "Write short reports on classroom experiments or projects" and "Write phrases or sentences for a group story or project with support or guidance."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual guided writing using a provided sentence starter or template.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher reminds students of the classroom recycling program and the theme "We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. The teacher provides a simple template on the board or a worksheet:
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>My Classroom Recycling Report</b><br>
                        Our class has a recycling program. We ____________ (what do you recycle?).<br>
                        There is a ____________ (color) bin for ____________ (what items?).<br>
                        We ____________ (how do you help the Earth?).</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Students complete the sentences, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced. They are encouraged to use words like "plastic," "bottles," "paper," "bin," "save," "protect," "environment," "easy." The teacher emphasizes that their sentences should be clear and describe their actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Students can draw a small picture next to their report to illustrate their recycling project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Students are discussing environmental actions by writing about them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Implicitly giving reasons for recycling (e.g., "to help the Earth").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during writing and a simple rubric for evaluating the written reports.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Classroom Recycling Report" from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric - A1.3):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., recycle, plastic, bottles, bin, environment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Uses present simple tense correctly in most sentences. Correct use of 'can' or 'there is/are'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Content:</b> Clearly describes a recycling action or purpose in 2-3 simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Spelling/Punctuation:</b> Basic spelling and punctuation are mostly accurate for familiar words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "Today, I wrote about ____________." (e.g., "recycling plastic bottles")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "One new word I used is ____________." (e.g., "environment" or "classify")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What was easy about writing today? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "How does writing about recycling help me remember it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., scaffolding with sentence frames, variety of practice activities).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve writing skills or engagement? (e.g., more direct vocabulary input, more peer correction, visual prompts for sentence generation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students meet the objective of writing simple reports and sentences about recycling?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students summarizing and integrating information about recycling. The writing practice from this lesson will help them formulate the sentences needed to explain their recycling project or poster to others during the mediation activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>